; REGRESSION FLAG for the nested / self-referential function-union case.
;
; `m1` is `merge`'s returned CLOSURE, whose body dispatches over its `fns`
; union. Feeding `m1` back in as a MEMBER of a second, same-signature merge
; (`[m1 c]`) makes the late (closure) function-union pass — which keys unions
; by structural signature — merge both arrays into ONE union that INCLUDES
; `m1`. Since `m1` dispatches over that very union, the generated dispatcher
; calls `m1` which calls the dispatcher: a real cycle in a call graph that
; easl requires to be a DAG. Today this stack-overflows the compiler.
;
; The program itself is well-defined and terminating:
;   m1(x) = fold [a b] = b(a(x)) = (2x)+1
;   m2(x) = fold [m1 c] = c(m1(x)) = ((2x)+1) - 1 = 2x
;   m2(3) = 6
;
; Making this compile + run needs the PER-USAGE late-pass rebuild
; (function-values-design.md §12): `[a b]` and `[m1 c]` must get DISTINCT
; enums keyed by member-set, so `m1` dispatches over `{a,b}`'s enum while being
; a member of `{m1,c}`'s enum — a DAG. Structural (signature) keying cannot
; represent this. See the `fn_value_nested_union_recursion` test wrapper.
(defn merge [fns: [2: (Fn [f32] f32)]]: (Fn [f32] f32)
  (fn [x]
    (let [@var q x]
      (for [i (array-length fns)]
        (= q ((fns i) q)))
      q)))

(defn f []: f32
  (let [a (fn [x] (* x 2.))
        b (fn [x] (+ x 1.))
        m1 (merge [a b])
        c (fn [x] (- x 1.))
        m2 (merge [m1 c])]
    (m2 3.)))
